**Theorem GAW9.** The following statements separate the unconditional
conclusions from the finite exclusion.

1. If $q=3$, $p=kq+1$ is prime, $k\ge2$, and
   $\rho(p)\mid\pi(q)$, then $k=2$.
2. If $q>5$ is prime, $k\ge4$, and $A_k(q)$ holds, then the divisibility
   alternative in GAW1 holds and

   $$
   p\mid F_kF_{k+2}F_{2(k-2)}.                         \tag{GAW8}
   $$

3. For every prime $q>5$, without $E_{200}$,

   $$
   \boxed{
   0\in S(q)\iff q_q=0\ \text{or}\ A_{\{2\}}(q)\ \text{or}\
   A_{[4,200]}(q)\ \text{or}\ A_{>200}(q).}           \tag{GAW9}
   $$

4. Under $E_{200}$, GAW2 follows in its stated range: for every odd prime
   $q\ne5$, every prime $p=kq+1$ with $2\le k\le200$ and
   $\rho(p)\mid\pi(q)$ has $k=2$. Under the same assumption, GAW9 reduces
   to GAW6. Thus the implication in GAW6 that excludes all intermediate
   multipliers and leaves only $k=2$ or $p>200q+1$ is conditional on
   $E_{200}$. The reverse implication from any branch displayed in GAW6
   to $0\in S(q)$ is unconditional.

In particular, GAW1, GAW4, GAW5, the four-branch equivalence GAW9, and the
theorem following GAW6 about external prime witnesses in residue classes do
not require $E_{200}$.

**Proof.** For $q=3$, one has $\pi(3)=8$ and $F_8=21$. Alignment makes
$p$ divide $F_8$, while $p=3k+1$ and $k\ge2$; the only possibility is
$p=7$ and $k=2$. This proves the first assertion without finite enumeration.

For $q>5$, assertion 2 is exactly the linear-index conclusion of GAW1 and
does not use a search over multipliers. Every aligned prime has
$p=kq+1$ with even $k\ge2$. Partitioning those even multipliers into
$k=2$, $4\le k\le200$, and $k>200$, and then applying GAW5 proves GAW9.
Assumption $E_{200}$ deletes precisely the middle alternative. Together
with the unconditional $q=3$ calculation, this gives the corrected
conditional form of GAW2 and the forward implication of GAW6. Conversely,
each remaining aligned-prime branch supplies the other-prime term in GAW5,
so the reverse implication needs no finite exclusion. Finally, GAW4-GAW5
and the residue-class theorem following GAW6 use the Fibonacci valuation
formula and Euler's product, not $E_{200}$; their conclusions therefore
remain unconditional.


